/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode() {}
 *     TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
 *     TreeNode(int val, TreeNode left, TreeNode right) {
 *         this.val = val;
 *         this.left = left;
 *         this.right = right;
 *     }
 * }
 */
class Solution {

    int idx;

    /**
     * Approach: the idea is take root from preorder[]. search that root inside inorder[]. all the elements to the left in inorder[] will be left subtree and right elements will be right subtree. Do this recursively.
     - In order to search root element in inorder[] we are using hashmap.
    */

    /*Time Complexity: O(N)
    HashMap Construction: We iterate through the inorder array of size N exactly once to populate the HashMap. This takes O(N) time.
    
    Tree Construction (Recursion): Eecursive helper function processes every node exactly once (tracked by the global idx moving from 0 to N-1). Inside the function, looking up the index in the HashMap and creating a new node both take O(1) time. Therefore, building the tree takes O(N) time.
    
    Total Time: O(N) + O(N) = O(N).
    
    */

    /*
    Space Complexity: O(N)
    - The HashMap: Storing all N nodes and their indices takes O(N) space.
    - The Call Stack (Implicit Space): Because this is a recursive solution, the system uses the call stack to   manage the function calls. The maximum depth of this stack is equal to the height of the tree (H).
    - In the worst-case scenario (a completely skewed tree, like a linked list), the call stack will go N levels deep, taking O(N) space.
    - In the best-case scenario (a perfectly balanced tree), the call stack takes O(log N) space.
    */
    public TreeNode buildTree(int[] preorder, int[] inorder) {
        this.idx = 0;
        //1. Create a map for o(1) search of root elemnt in inorder[]
        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < inorder.length; i++) {
            map.put(inorder[i], i);
        }
        return helper(preorder, 0, inorder.length - 1, map);
    }

    //Preorder --> root -left-right
    //Preorder --> left -root-right
    private TreeNode helper(int[] preorder, int inStart, int inEnd, Map<Integer, Integer> inOrderMap) {
        //1. Base case
        //end index should be greter than start index
        if (inStart > inEnd)
            return null;

        //2. Logic
        //In PreOrder traversal first node is root node. Create root node
        int rootNodeVal = preorder[idx];
        idx++;
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(rootNodeVal);

        //search root value index inside inorder[]
        int inRootIndx = inOrderMap.get(rootNodeVal);

        //3. Recursion
        //In inorder traversal left subtree will be from inStart to the rootIndex-1;
        root.left = helper(preorder, inStart, inRootIndx - 1, inOrderMap);

        //In inorder traversal right subtree will be from inRootIndx+1 to the inEnd
        root.right = helper(preorder, inRootIndx + 1, inEnd, inOrderMap);

        return root;

    }
}